            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "The design of 'mini.clue' is pretty much established. It implements a custom key query process that narrows down mappings and/or user clues for the same mode. Here is [a help entry](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/d8faae693d266ee4f3a2bf1fb7ca4bc81314c3e9/doc/mini-clue.txt#L169-L171).\r\n\r\nSetup of 'mini.clue' is intentionally opt-in, i.e. users need to explicitly set up which clues they want to show. Here is [an example if you don't know where to start](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/d8faae693d266ee4f3a2bf1fb7ca4bc81314c3e9/doc/mini-clue.txt#L241-L284). For example, it defines `g` as trigger and after pressing `g` shows all built-in mappings that start with it (including `g?`; you may need to scroll the clue window with default `<C-d>`).\r\n\r\nThe `d` and `y` are operators which change mode from Normal to Operator-pending and wait for keys in *that* mode. Showing clue window right after Neovim starts waiting for a textobject/motion was considered, but did not really fit the 'mini.clue' implementation. See #1008.",
